> The PANACEA project is organised around six complementary Work Packages (WP), described below. WP1 Stakeholder Engagement shapes the various analyses with a broad co-design process, to ensure that the assumptions, scenarios, and outputs across the projects are appropriate to the intended users. WP2 - WP5 are sector-specific projects aimed at developing new methods to bridge disciplines and thereby better understand risks and adaptation options. WP6 Impact Synthesis brings together these new estimates with existing research to develop tools and datasets that can better inform stakeholders and low- and lower-middle income country (LLMIC) policy makers.
> 
> WP1. Stakeholder Co-design
> OBJECTIVES: Ensure that stakeholder views are incorporated into the project-wide analysis, through scenario and question development, identification of outcomes of interest; and engagement around results and policy directions.
> 
> PANACEA is a stakeholder-oriented project, with frequent interaction with local stakeholders to shape the project analysis and outcomes. The primary stakeholders for PANACEA are national-level policy makers, including representatives from Ministries of Finance and environment agencies. These will form (1) country-specific and project-wide advisory groups, and the core of (2) stakeholder engagement workshops occurring regularly during the project (see Gantt Chart). Building on the PIs existing relationships, the stakeholders will include:
> UK agency representatives for His Majesty's Treasury (HMT), Office of Budget Responsibility (OBR), and the Department for Environment, Food & Rural Affairs (Defra).
> Nigeria representatives of National Space Research and Development Agency (NASRDA) and the Food and Nutrition Technical Assistance Project (FANTA).
> Representatives of their Brazilian Ministry of Finance and the Novo Brasil initiative.
> 
> METHODS: The project-wide advisory group will include representatives of the (three) country-specific advisory groups. Each of the four advisory groups will meet quarterly virtually, and in-person workshops will be convened twice for each country (the PI has organised two large workshops on improving climate risk assessments, leading to Rising et al. 2022 and Royal Society 2023). Key decisions points include:
> Project-Wide decisions: Identify climate scenarios and global economic baselines: The PI has used off-the-shelf Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs), Network for Greening the Financial System (NGFS) scenarios, and probabilistic Resources for the Future Socioeconomic Projections (RFF-SPs) in past work, but stakeholder engagement under a current project suggests that policy makers are more interested in "most likely" and "worst case" scenarios.
> Country-specific decisions: (1) Develop risk and adaptation storylines: Domestic and international storylines for each country will provide context and applications for the quantitative risk outcomes and adaptation models (WP2 - 5 and WP6). These will be developed through workshops. Meetings will also discuss the significance of results to active policy discussions. (2) Design interactive visualisations to inform decision-making: Interactive displays will allow stakeholders to explore risks across dimensions of region, time, scenario, and sector, and the benefits of adaptation, across adaptation solutions and investment plans. Workshops will be used to design and refine interfaces to the project dataset.
> 
> WP2. GDP & Supply Chains
> POLICY OBJECTIVES: Estimate how each country's economic growth depends upon weather and climate across the globe, and use it to better project future losses.
> METHOD CONTRIBUTION: Combining trade models with best practice climate econometrics.
> 
> Three important lines of evidence on the potential for losses to GDP from climate change are brought together here. (1) Econometric analyses have shown that temperature shocks undermine economic growth, with Burke et al. (2015) perhaps the best known example of a deep literature that has explored the role of different estimation strategies, weather shocks, and persistence. These studies study only the role of local weather, as a causal identification strategy, despite the tight interconnections of the global economic system. (2) Recent studies have applied similar methods to global temperature shocks (Bilal & Känzig 2024, Neal et al. 2025), and find much larger losses. While these are attributed to the globally correlated nature of climate shocks, they rely on worryingly strong identification assumptions. (3) Alternatively, computable general equilibrium (CGE) and related trade models are regularly used to study economy-wide losses associated with an enumeration of sector-specific impacts and how these impacts are transmitted across supply-chains.
> 
> METHODS (collaborators Ralf Martin & Mirabelle Muuls, Imperial):
> Identify best practices from the GDP growth literature, informed by cross-validation: This draws on a systematic meta-analysis of the GDP growth literature recently completed by the PI (Rising 2023 (public report); under review at Nature), filtering through over 44,000 papers, and projecting 130 models from 32 papers. We will evaluate the theoretical and empirical foundation of approaches to handle precipitation (e.g., Kotz et al. 2022), variability (Linsenmeier et al. 2023), and outliers (Jiao et al. 2024). We will also draw upon WP3 results to explore the inclusion of water security (e.g., Brown et al. 2013). We will evaluate models on their ability to describe growth rates in years not observed in the original papers.
> Use Lasso methods applied to "foreign" weather predictors to estimate trade spillovers for each country: The initial approach will include weather in other countries into the best-practice econometric models, and then apply Lasso to determine the most predictive bilateral connections (that is, all instances where country B's weather usefully informs growth in country A). This provides a kind of "economic footprint" for each country.
> Improve interpretability and empirical-macro insights by studying structural constraints on spillovers: Useful insights will come from answers to the following research questions: (1) Does the strength of the spillover effect relate reliably to the amount of trade between two countries? Is that relationship stronger for trade in particular sectors (e.g., agriculture)? (2) How do spillover effects propagate through the global trade network? That is, how are impacts on the trade partners of one's trade partners felt? To answer these, we will apply constraints on the bilateral weather relationships based on trade data in the Eora Global Supply Chain Database (Lenzen et al. 2013) and composite indices, based on trade flows as in Acemoglu et al. (2016).
> 
> WP3. River & Surface Flooding
> POLICY OBJECTIVE: Develop high-quality estimates of economic losses due to surface and river flooding from climate change across the globe, and study how zoning laws (understood broadly as local restrictions on the population and capital growth) can reduce future damages.
> METHOD CONTRIBUTIONS: Develop (1) synthetic flow predictors for use in empirical risk analysis, (2) a theoretically-grounded approach to accounting for spatial correlation, and (3) an outcomes-based inference of the spatial distribution of capital at risk.
> 
> Flood impacts on infrastructure, housing, and businesses are reliably among the highest sources of damage from process-based assessments. However, economic damages from flooding remains a major gap for both econometrics and global process-based approaches: Flood risk estimates at a reasonable global resolution is lacking, with recent attempts assuming uniformly distributed capital within each country (Willner et al. 2018).
> 
> The recent rise in damages from flooding is not, in many regions, coming from increased rainfall. A major driver is the increase in capital and population in high-risk areas, particularly in rapidly growing peri-urban areas. We propose to study restrictions on capital and population density and flood-management dams (essentially, modifying the exposure) as adaptations.
> 
> METHODS (collaborators Ashwin Rode, Chicago & Laureline Josset, Columbia): 
> Construct an exogenous predictor of flow volume: My key innovation for this WP is to develop a synthetic flow predictor, based on hydrological modeling, to provide a better independent hazard variable for use in econometric analysis. The flow predictor is based on how river networks accumulate runoff and snow melt, using globally consistent tools like TauDEM (Tarboton et al. 2009). We can either use reported run-off and melt index coefficients, calibrate them with the global network of NOAA and RivDis gauges, or calibrate them in the full damage estimation process. The main results will use the final option.
> Study reported flooding losses using the flow predictor: We will use a hurdle model to explain outcomes as a nonlinear function of synthetic flow. Damage outcomes will come from EM-DAT (after 2000 when biases are less) and a global collection of country meteorological offices reported flood damages. Figure 2 shows EM-DAT and MetOffice estimates of damage estimates for the UK. Synthetic flows will be aggregated to the reporting unit level using multiple datasets, including the Global Human Settlement Layer, time-varying nightlights, and infrastructure estimates from the GRI Risk Viewer (Russell et al. 2025).
> Solve the problem of hydrologically-correlated error terms: This is a structured correlation problem, but the expected strength of upstream correlation is an empirical outcome. An analysis of these structures will support a wide range of related work.
> Evaluate the role of land use and dams: The same datasets used for spatial aggregate (step 2) provide covariates to study the drivers of risk (methods from Carleton et al. 2022). We will look at how this relationship is mediated by time-varying total upstream dam capacity from the Global Reservoir and Dam Database. This step will draw upon the spatial-dependence methods from WP2.
> 
> WP4. Perennial Crops & Management
> POLICY OBJECTIVE: Develop the first empirical, global estimates of perennial crop losses under climate change, and how management can avoid these losses.
> METHOD CONTRIBUTIONS: Integrate process-based results into an empirical framework that accounts for hidden variables.
> 
> This work package extends two ground-breaking projects of the PI's, both of which integrate process-based outcomes into an empirical framework to better study climate impacts on agriculture. (1) Rising (2024) provides a framework for studying the hidden role of perennial management (particularly harvesting decisions), which is crucial for understanding their yield outcomes. This approach was extended globally for coffee in Sachs et al (2019), including estimates of the benefits of management (see Figure 3). (2) Unpublished work (Rising & Tahmid WP) directly incorporates three agricultural process-based model outputs into an econometric framework, producing higher performance than either the empirical model or process-based models under cross-validation, and more credibly capturing nonlinearities in biophysical processes and the effects of hidden farmer management.
> 
> This work package would study three cash crops as case studies to address perennials generally: coffee, cocoa, and tea have similar market dynamics and span a range of perennial crop types.
> 
> METHODS (collaborator Muhammad Taky Tahmid, Delaware):
> Construct a global dataset of observed yields, growing areas, process-based outputs and suitability, and agronomically-relevant weather variables: Yield is available at the subnational level for coffee (8 countries, 73% of global production), cocoa (at least 4 countries, 64% of global production), tea (at least 4 countries, 66% of global production), as well as at the national level globally, with most of these sources available for 20 years or more. High resolution maps of the geography of these crops within the regional units will be used to specify weather grid cells to predict each one.
> Integrate the methods in Rising (2024), Sachs et al (2019), and Rising & Tahmid (WP): The econometric approach follows Hultgren et al. (2025), which integrated with process-based outcomes in Rising & Tahmid (WP) and suitability outcomes in Sachs et al (2019). However, perennial crops suffer from lack of data and misreporting of biophysical yields (production divided by planted area), and observed yields (production divided by harvested area) are unreliable and biased (Rising 2024). The solution in Rising (2024) extends econometrics with a Bayesian MCMC to calibrate the model with hidden variables for planting and management.
> Study margins of adaptation: Several margins of adaptation are exposed by the model, and will be studied, and specifically drivers behind model-estimated variation in weather sensitivity, costs of harvest, and replanting rates, across regions; and the role of irrigation and fertilizer use, based on process-based outputs.
> 
> WP5. Biodiversity & Conservation
> POLICY OBJECTIVES: Identify species at risk of extinction and the characteristics of their biology and nearby human activity associated with high risk. Translate these into improved maps of conservation priorities.
> METHOD CONTRIBUTION: Integrate robust decision-making into a land use optimisation model, to account for the dynamic, uncertain evolution of extinction risk.
> 
> The project relies on a unique dataset: the PI has collected copies of the IUCN Red List database regularly since 2010, planning this analysis for the last 15 years (in 2010, 2013, 2015, 2017, 2020, 2022, and 2025). The Red List reports for each species an endangerment status, population trend, and spatial distribution. Figure 1 shows how plant species have transitioned between risk categories from 2010 and 2025.
> 
> The central modeling assumption is that traditional risk factors, such as land use changes and species characteristics, effectively “select” a risk model for each species-location, but weather shocks contribute to the actual category transitions (i.e., from endangered to extinct-in-the-wild).
> 
> METHODS:
> Use an ordered logit model to simultaneously fit weather shocks impacts and risk models: For a linear model, we have yitOrderedLogit(βk(i,t)Wit+αi+δt,{θc }) where yit is a categorical outcome (either Red List status or population trend) for a species i in year t. βk(i,t) is the risk coefficient for that species, which depends on species characteristics and land use change, under a hierarchical relationship (not shown). Wit could include temperatures, heat waves, and droughts. {θc } is the central value for each of the ordered categories (e.g., {θVulnerable, θEndangered, θExtinct}).
> Construct improved willingness-to-pay (WTP) valuations by species, drawing on benefit transfer approaches: We will analyse meta-analysis datasets like Johnston et al. (2006) and Nobel et al. (2020) to generalise WTP values across species and regions. To rationalise this data, we build on Noack et al. (2025), by calibrating a level of "substitutability" between species that simultaneously predicts the WTP results for species abundance, species richness, and individual species/habitats.
> Incorporate these dynamic risks into a spatial optimization framework, accounting for robust decision-making: Here, we build a model to identify areas for biodiversity conservation, accounting for future evolution of land use change (multiple scenarios, building on Taylor et al. 2021) and uncertainty in transitions and WTP estimates. This model will use linear optimisation techniques that maximise total conservation benefit, drawing upon Polasky et al. (2008) and Rising (2020).
> 
> WP6. Impacts Synthesis
> OBJECTIVES: Provide total economic risk estimates at a subnational level and develop an interactive tool for decision-makers to explore adaptation investment pathways.
> 
> The outputs of this project, combined with empirical results from the Climate Impact Lab and biophysical process model results from IIASA, offer a potential sea-change in the assessment of vulnerabilities in poor and data-poor regions. Two interfaces will be developed, based on discussions with WP1 LLMIC stakeholders, building on an existing model the PI is developing for the UK. The tool will allow decision-makers to develop "adaptation pathways" (Werners et al. 2021), and visualise costs and benefits of different adaptation investment plans.
> 
> METHODS (collaborator Nicola Ranger, LSE):
> Develop emulators of outcomes from PANACEA and from collaborating groups: This work builds upon three existing projects by the PI: (1) a project within the Climate Impact Lab to emulate risk relationships, (2) a Climate and Clean Air Coalition (CCAC) report to emulate climate risks from the broader literature, and (3) near-comprehensive estimates of climate risk in the United Kingdom (Rising et al. 2022c (public report), under review at Nature Climate Change). These light-weight emulators reproduce risk and adaptation results from existing models, allowing them to be projected under custom scenarios.
> Integrate emulators into the Policy Analysis of the Greenhouse Effect (PAGE) model: The PAGE model (Waidelich et al. 2024) is a state-of-the-art integrated assessment model with risks, adaptation, and mitigation, which has been integrated with the FaIR climate model (Leach et al. 2021) and disaggregated to the country level under the CCAC project. Interactions between adaptation, mitigation, development, catastrophic change, and a range of policy levers will be explored under the new WP1 scenarios.
> Develop an interface to WP2 - 5 data and for creating adaptation pathways: Through workshops coordinated with WP1, we will develop two new interfaces on an existing decision-support tool to identify risks globally and allow stakeholders to explore adaptation investment plans.
> 
