> <ins>**WP4. Perennial Crops & Management**</ins>
> **POLICY OBJECTIVE:** Develop first empirical, global estimates of perennial crop losses under climate change, focusing on management's role in avoiding these losses.
> **METHOD CONTRIBUTIONS:** Integrate process-based results into an empirical framework that accounts for hidden variables.
> 
> This work package extends two of the PI's groundbreaking projects, both integrating process-based outcomes into an empirical framework to improve studying climate impacts on agriculture. (1) Rising (2024) provides a framework to study hidden perennial management roles, crucial to understanding yield outcomes. This was extended globally for coffee in Sachs et al (2019), including management benefits estimates (see Figure 3). (2) Unpublished work (Rising & Tahmid WP) directly incorporates outputs from three agricultural process-based models into an econometric framework, yielding higher performance and superior nonlinear biophysical process capture compared to stand-alone models.
> 
> We will study three cash crops—coffee, cocoa, and tea—as case studies representative of perennial crops.
> 
> **METHODS** (collaborator Muhammad Taky Tahmid, Delaware):
> * Construct a global dataset of observed yields, growing areas, process-based outputs, and relevant agronomical weather variables. Yield data is available for coffee (8 countries, 73% of production), cocoa (at least 4 countries, 64%), tea (at least 4 countries, 66%), and nationally with a span of 20+ years, using high resolution crop geography maps to specify predictive weather grid cells.
> * Integrate methods in Rising (2024), Sachs et al (2019), and Rising & Tahmid (WP) using an approach by Hultgren et al. (2025), incorporating process-based outcomes and suitability outcomes. Perennial crops have data and misreporting issues for biophysical and observed yields (Rising 2024), addressed by a Bayesian MCMC calibration modeling hidden variables for planting and management.
> * Study adaptation margins and the model-estimated variation in weather sensitivity, harvest costs, and replanting rates, across regions; as well as irrigation and fertilizer intervention roles with process-based outputs.
> 
> <ins>**WP5. Biodiversity & Conservation**</ins>
> **POLICY OBJECTIVES:** Identify species at risk of extinction and the biological and human activity characteristics associated with high risk. Translate these insights into improved conservation priority maps.
> **METHOD CONTRIBUTION:** Integrate robust decision-making into a land use optimization model to account for the dynamic, uncertain evolution of extinction risk.
> 
> The project uses a unique dataset: the PI has collected IUCN Red List database copies regularly since 2010, planning this analysis for 15 years (2010, 2013, 2015, 2017, 2020, 2022, and 2025). The Red List provides species endangerment status, population trends, and spatial distribution (see Figure 1 for plant species risk category transitions from 2010 to 2025).
> 
> The primary modeling assumption is that traditional risk factors, such as land use changes and species characteristics, effectively “select” a risk model for each species-location, with weather shocks contributing to category transitions (e.g., from endangered to extinct-in-the-wild).
> 
> **METHODS:**
> * Use an ordered logit model to assess weather shocks' impacts and risk models: For a linear model, we use yitOrderedLogit(βk(i,t)Wit+αi+δt,{θc}) where yit represents a species i's categorical outcome in year t. βk(i,t) represents the risk coefficient for that species, dependent on species characteristics and land use change, under a hierarchical structure (not shown). Wit could include temperatures, heat waves, and droughts. {θc} denotes the central threshold for ordered categories (e.g., {θVulnerable, θEndangered, θExtinct}).
> * Construct improved WTP valuations by species, using benefit transfer approaches. We will analyze meta-analysis datasets like Johnston et al. (2006) and Nobel et al. (2020) for generalizing WTP values across species and regions. Building on Noack et al. (2025), we rationalize these data, calibrating species substitutability levels to simultaneously predict WTP results for species abundance, richness, and individual species/habitats.
> * Incorporate these dynamic risks into a spatial optimization framework, accommodating robust decision-making. A model is built to identify biodiversity conservation areas, considering future land use change scenarios and transitions and WTP estimate uncertainties. This model will use Polasky et al. (2008) and Rising (2020)-inspired linear optimization techniques, maximizing conservation benefit.
> 
> <ins>**WP6. Impacts Synthesis**</ins>
> **OBJECTIVES:** Deliver total economic risk estimates at a subnational level and develop an interactive tool enabling decision-makers to explore adaptation investment pathways.
> 
> The project's outputs, merged with empirical Climate Impact Lab results and biophysical IIASA process model outcomes, could revolutionize assessing vulnerabilities in poor, data-poor regions. Two interfaces will be developed, informed by discussions with WP1 LLMIC stakeholders, based on a model in development by the PI for the UK. The tool allows decision-makers to develop "adaptation pathways" (Werners et al. 2021) and visualize adaptation investment plan costs and benefits.
> 
> **METHODS** (collaborator Nicola Ranger, LSE):
> * Develop outcomes emulators from PANACEA and collaborating groups: This builds on three PI projects: (1) an initiative within the Climate Impact Lab to emulate risk relationships, (2) a Climate and Clean Air Coalition (CCAC) report to emulate climate risks from the broader literature, and (3) near-comprehensive climate risk estimates for the UK (Rising et al. 2022c). These lightweight emulators reproduce risk and adaptation results from existing models, enabling scenario-based projections.
> * Integrate emulators into the Policy Analysis of the Greenhouse Effect (PAGE) model. The state-of-the-art PAGE model (Waidelich et al. 2024), integrated with the FaIR climate model (Leach et al. 2021) and disaggregated to country level under the CCAC project, will explore adaptation, mitigation, development, catastrophic change, and policy lever interactions under the new WP1 scenarios.
> * Develop an interface for WP2 - 5 data and adaptation pathway creation, creating new interface iterations on an existing decision-support tool to identify global risks and allow stakeholders to explore adaptation investment plans through workshops coordinated with WP1.
